-- ── VII. THE ABSTRACTION: SHADOW/MIRROR ARE SPECIES OF ASYMMETRY/SYMMETRY ────
--
-- PROVENANCE, and it UPGRADES section VI. Stated by Tyler directly to this
-- session on 2026-09-21, in two messages, the second correcting the first:
--
--   "I do think hyperlogic is closer to hyperethics, maybe hyperlogic is a
--    symmetry with hypermath, hyperethics is asymmetry of hyperlogic,
--    hyperethics is still a shadow of hypermath, so we can interchange shadow
--    forms with asymmetric shaped forms, and mirror forms with symmetric
--    shaped forms. This seems to be some sort of universal
--    transition/translation norm"
--
--   "not interchange, abstract"
--
-- Section VI's USER-DECLARED-AS-REPORTED caveat is now PARTIALLY LIFTED. The
-- mirror limb (hypermath with hyperlogic) and shadow legs 1 and 2 are restated
-- here FIRST-HAND. Leg 3 -- shadow(hyperphysics) = hyperethics -- was NOT
-- restated and keeps the relayed caveat.

-- d-abstraction-is-not-interchange : his own correction, and it is the load-
--   bearing half of the statement, so it goes first.
--   INTERCHANGE would say: wherever the corpus reads `shadow`, one may write
--   `asymmetric form` salva veritate. That is a synonym claim, refuted by one
--   asymmetric relation that is not a shadow.
--   ABSTRACT says: shadow is ONE SPECIES of asymmetric form; mirror is ONE
--   SPECIES of symmetric form; the genus is what carries the norm.
--   Strictly weaker on each instance, strictly stronger as a program -- because
--   it PREDICTS that the corpus contains asymmetric relations that are not
--   shadows and symmetric relations that are not mirrors. That prediction is
--   testable, and section `d-the-corpus-already-sorts-by-symmetry` tests it.

-- d-which-sense-of-symmetric : three readings are available and they do not
--   agree. Naming the choice and the reason for it, not just the choice.
--
--   READING A -- profile balance, a UNARY property of one field.
--     symmetric = interior:boundary balanced; asymmetric = one-sided.
--     Fits "hyperlogic is a symmetry" (6/8, the only balanced field). But
--     Tyler's sentences are relational -- "symmetry WITH hypermath",
--     "asymmetry OF hyperlogic" -- so A mis-types the arity of both.
--
--   READING B -- profile matching between TWO fields.
--     symmetric pair = same profile; asymmetric pair = complementary.
--     hypermath 17/0 against hyperethics 0/45 is perfectly complementary, so
--     leg 1 holds. But hypermath 17/0 against hyperlogic 6/8 is neither same
--     nor complementary, so B CONTRADICTS his claim that the two are a
--     symmetry. B is the reading section VI was implicitly using.
--
--   READING C -- the relation-theoretic sense, and the one taken.
--     symmetric = the relation holds in both directions; asymmetric = it holds
--     one way only.
--       mirror  A and B reflecting off ONE ANOTHER is mutual -> SYMMETRIC
--       shadow  a body casts a shadow; a shadow casts no body -> ASYMMETRIC
--
--   WHY C AND NOT A OR B. Not because it is the flattering one. Because C is
--   the only reading under which `d-mirror-changed-arity` -- measured in
--   section VI BEFORE this abstraction existed, from an independent statement
--   -- is PREDICTED rather than coincidental. Symmetric relations are
--   naturally binary and unordered; asymmetric ones are naturally directed.
--   A and B have to treat the arity split as an unrelated fact about two
--   English words. C derives it. That is the discriminating test between
--   three readings of one sentence, and it is the whole justification.

-- d-the-corpus-already-sorts-by-symmetry : the confirmation, and it is
--   stronger than the statement it confirms.
--   Measured 2026-09-21, scope: *.hm and *.md in each field's own tree,
--   `references/` and `.git/` excluded.
--
--     field                symmetr*   asymmetr*   (files mentioning)
--     hypermath                  38          16
--     hyperethics                14          13
--     metamathethicology          4           2
--     hyperlogic                  3           2
--     hyperphysics                0           0
--
--   The COUNT is not the finding -- 38 files looks like a vocabulary
--   collision, and on the count alone I would have ruled these words taken.
--   Reading the sense inverts the verdict. hypermath uses symmetric in
--   EXACTLY reading C, and PROVES it:
--       L0_ground.hm:541   "The derives relation is not symmetric."
--       L1_relations.hm:57  derive similar-symmetric as FORM: x ~~ y -> y ~~ x
--                [CORRECTED in section VIII -- was cited as L0:57]
--       L1_relations.hm:351 "Directional asymmetry is established: derives
--                 is not symmetric."
--       L0_ground.hm:540    derive derives-is-directional as FORM
--       L0_ground.hm:663    step 40 defers it to FRAME/L1
--       L1_relations.hm:327 discharges it
--                [CORRECTED in section VIII -- was cited as L1:476 alone]
--       L2_operations.hm:430 abstracted-path-is-reflexive-and-symmetric -- ~=
--                is reflexive and symmetric on DerivationPath.
--                [~= IS THE ABSTRACTION OPERATOR. See section VIII.]
--       L3_ordinatics.hm:404 "== is not symmetric in general"
--
--   So THE HYPERMATH QUADRILATERAL IS ALREADY SORTED BY SYMMETRY, and the
--   sort is discharged as FORM derivations:
--       ~~   similar          SYMMETRIC, proved
--       ~=   abstracted path  SYMMETRIC, proved
--       ==   identity         not symmetric in general
--       ->   derives          ASYMMETRIC, proved
--
--   That is the prediction of d-abstraction-is-not-interchange, satisfied:
--   `derives` is an asymmetric relation that is NOT a shadow, and `~~` is a
--   symmetric relation that is NOT a mirror. The genus is populated beyond the
--   two species. Tyler's abstraction is therefore not new vocabulary being
--   imported -- it is the NAME OF A CLASSIFICATION HYPERMATH ALREADY PROVES AT
--   L0 AND L1 and never named. Adopting symmetric/asymmetric is REUSE in the
--   established sense, not overloading.
--
--   hyperethics uses the same abstract sense on different objects -- proof
--   asymmetry ("a single countermodel REFUTES conclusively; that asymmetry is
--   carried in the return types"), the bridge asymmetry, the two-register
--   asymmetry. The MODALITY differs and that difference is itself a datum:
--   hypermath PROVES its symmetry facts as FORM; hyperethics REPORTS its
--   asymmetries as findings ("the bridge came out asymmetric, and that was
--   left as a finding"). Proved against found.

-- c-leg-2-verdict-withdrawn : mine, and it does not survive the abstraction.
--   Section VI recorded shadow(hyperlogic) = hyperethics as FAILS, on the
--   ground that hyperlogic carries both interior and boundary and so "cannot
--   be the body of a shadow; it is where the shadow is SEEN."
--   That is a READING-B argument -- it is entirely about profiles. Under
--   reading C, profiles are IRRELEVANT to whether a relation is asymmetric;
--   only directionality is. So the refutation loses its ground.
--   Leg 2 goes back to OPEN. Not confirmed -- reopened. The verdict was
--   reading-dependent and section VI did not say which reading it used, which
--   is the same defect this file has now caught twice.
--
--   AND THE SAME NUMBER CHANGES SIDES. The fact that killed leg 2 under my
--   reading -- hyperlogic boundary 8 EXCEEDS interior 6 -- is measured support
--   for his other claim, that "hyperlogic is closer to hyperethics": on the
--   interior:boundary profile hyperlogic (6/8, boundary-led) sits on
--   hyperethics' side of the divide (0/45) and not hypermath's (17/0). One
--   measurement, two claims, opposite service.

-- d-shadow-is-not-a-function : forced by taking legs 1 and 2 together.
--   shadow(hypermath) = hyperethics AND shadow(hyperlogic) = hyperethics means
--   hyperethics has TWO bodies. Asymmetry permits this -- an asymmetric
--   relation may be many-to-one. But this file is written throughout as though
--   shadow(X) picks out a unique Y, and that is now wrong. Shadow is a
--   RELATION, not a function. Two bodies, one shadow.
--   Nothing above needs rewriting for this; the sentences that assume
--   functionality need FLAGGING when they are next relied on.

-- d-translation-test-is-decidable : the operational payoff, and the reason the
--   abstraction is worth more than the two species were.
--   He gave two words, transition AND translation, and they are not the same.
--   Read TRANSLATION: carrying a statement from one field's language into
--   another's. Then the norm says
--       a translation between two fields is SYMMETRIC iff they mirror,
--       ASYMMETRIC iff one shadows the other.
--   Which converts an unmeasurable question -- "is hyperethics the shadow of
--   hyperlogic?" -- into a PROCEDURE: take a hyperethics obligation, carry it
--   back into hyperlogic, and see whether the reverse carry survives. Reverse
--   works -> symmetric -> mirror. Reverse fails -> asymmetric -> shadow.
--   The corpus is large enough to run this. It is the first test of any leg
--   here that does not depend on a keyword census.

-- nd-universality-is-not-free : it is NOT derivable that every inter-field
--   relation is symmetric or asymmetric.
--   A binary relation may be NEITHER -- mutual on one pair, one-way on
--   another. (It can be both only if empty.) So "universal norm" is not a
--   logical truth dressed as a claim; it is the substantive assertion that
--   inter-field derivation is HOMOGENEOUS IN DIRECTION. That is falsifiable by
--   exhibiting one field pair whose carrying works in both directions for some
--   statements and one direction for others -- and `==`, already recorded as
--   "not symmetric IN GENERAL" while bidirectional at cycles, is the first
--   place to look for exactly that.

-- nd-species-do-not-exhaust-genus : it is NOT derivable, and he did not claim
--   it, that shadow and mirror are the ONLY species. `derives` and `~~` are
--   already counterexamples inside hypermath. Any future claim of the form
--   "this relation is asymmetric, therefore it is a shadow" is the interchange
--   reading he explicitly withdrew.

-- GC-DS-1  [RUN 2026-09-21 -- see section IX. Asymmetry measured, witness NOT
--           supplied; both targets pre-refuse a shape-only witness.]
--          Run the translation test on one concrete pair: carry a hyperethics
--          obligation into hyperlogic and back. Settles leg 2 by procedure
--          rather than by census. This is now the cheapest open item here.
-- GC-DS-2  Flag every sentence in sections I-VI that treats shadow as a
--          function before relying on it. Two bodies, one shadow.
-- GC-DS-3  Re-run the symmetry census when hyperphysics is written. At 0/0 it
--          is the only field with no opinion about symmetry at all, and it is
--          the field the mirror is supposed to yield.


-- ── VIII. ABSTRACTION IS `~=`, NOT AN ENGLISH WORD ──────────────────────────
--
-- Tyler, 2026-09-21, after reading section VII:
--   "We use the quadrilateral filtration which includes abstraction or did you
--    miss the whole fucking point"
--
-- I did. Section VII treats "abstract, not interchange" as a distinction
-- between synonymy and genus/species and spends eighty lines on it in English.
-- The corpus has a FORMAL OPERATOR for exactly that move, authored by him,
-- specified, Lean-formalized and implemented. Worse: section VII CITES that
-- operator -- `abstracted-path-is-reflexive-and-symmetric` -- as evidence for
-- something else, and never notices that ~= IS ABSTRACTION. I quoted the tool
-- while looking for the tool.

-- d-the-quadrilateral-is-the-frame : source is hypermath's own
--   docs/research/QUADRILATERAL_FILTRATION.md, "Author: Tyler Roost", plus
--   lean4/Hypermath/QuadrilateralFiltration.lean and
--   src/hypermath_foundations/abstraction.py ("quadrilateral filtration and
--   transitive abstraction (~=)").
--
--   The old three-tier chain was linear:
--       Syntax (~~)  ->  Substance (=~)  ->  Semantics (==)
--   whose bottleneck is that every derivation must pass through concrete
--   substance before reaching semantic simulation. The quadrilateral adds the
--   ABSTRACTIVE OFF-BRANCH:
--       Syntax (~~)  --~=->  Abstraction (~=)
--   which, quoting the specification, "Discards operational run-time traces
--   while PRESERVING STRUCTURAL AND DEDUCTIVE INVARIANTS."
--   And the way back:
--       Abstraction (~=) --retrace[W_substance]-> Semantics (==)
--   conditioned: "An abstract equivalence A ~= B induces semantic simulation
--   A == B IF AND ONLY IF there exists a valid substance witness W certifying
--   that the abstraction is non-vacuously satisfiable in ground substance
--   without contradiction."

-- d-not-interchange-is-a-typed-move : his correction reads exactly onto two
--   nodes of his own diagram, and nothing about it is informal.
--       INTERCHANGE  =  ==   Semantics / simulation. Substitutable.
--       ABSTRACT     =  ~=   Abstraction. Traces discarded, invariants kept.
--   "Not interchange, abstract" is therefore not a hedge and not a philosophy-
--   of-language remark. It is a REFUSAL TO TAKE THE RETRACE WITHOUT A WITNESS.
--   shadow ~= asymmetric-form is asserted at the abstraction node; promoting it
--   to shadow == asymmetric-form requires W, and no W has been produced.
--   Section VII got the conclusion right and the type wrong, which is the
--   weaker half of being right.

-- d-the-census-measured-what-abstraction-discards : and THIS, not disjointness,
--   is why every leg verdict falls.
--
--   The peer session argued: disjointness is a symmetric predicate, so a
--   profile census cannot witness an asymmetric relation in either direction,
--   therefore leg 1's HOLDS falls with leg 2's FAILS. The CONCLUSION IS RIGHT
--   and I accept it. THE REASON IS TOO NARROW, and the narrow reason licenses a
--   wrong repair.
--
--   Too narrow because a profile is not a symmetric object: (17 interior,
--   0 boundary) against (0 interior, 45 boundary) does distinguish which field
--   is all-interior from which is all-boundary. Direction IS available in the
--   coordinates. What is missing is any argument that interior-dominance means
--   bodyhood -- an unargued interpretive step, not a structural impossibility.
--
--   The real reason is the filtration. The census counts DECLARATIONS. Counts
--   are trace volume; L2_operations.hm:248 says ~= is "abstracted from -- TRACE
--   VOLUME FORGOTTEN", and :27 that it "drops exactly the quantification".
--   So the census measures precisely the quantity that abstraction is DEFINED
--   TO DISCARD. The legs are claims at the Abstraction node; the census is a
--   measurement on the Syntax->Substance concrete path. They are on different
--   branches of the quadrilateral. No amount of counting could ever have borne
--   on them, in either direction, for either leg.
--
--   CARRIER CONFIRMS IT INDEPENDENTLY. ~= is declared on DerivationPath
--   (L2_operations.hm:241) and "has no L1 counterpart" (:253). The census
--   measures declarations on Forms -- L1 objects. Wrong carrier, stated in the
--   source, before any of this.

-- c-drop-the-numbers-keep-the-kinds : the peer proposed "keep the numbers, drop
--   the verdicts". Under the filtration it is the other way round.
--   The specification says the off-branch discards run-time traces while
--   PRESERVING STRUCTURAL INVARIANTS. Presence or absence of a declaration KIND
--   is structural and survives ~=. How many there are is volume and does not.
--     survives  : hypermath exhibits no boundary kind; hyperethics no interior
--                 kind; hyperlogic exhibits both.
--     forgotten : 17, 45, 8, 6.
--   So the durable content of three censuses is a KIND TABLE, and every
--   integer in sections VI and VII is below the abstraction line. The numbers
--   were never the instrument -- and now that is derivable rather than a
--   lesson learned twice by accident.
--   It does NOT rescue leg 1: kind-presence disjointness is still symmetric, so
--   direction is still unwitnessed. All three legs OPEN. Agreed with the peer,
--   on better ground.

-- d-the-translation-test-is-the-retrace : GC-DS-1 was proposed in section VII
--   as "the cheapest open item". It is more than that, and the quadrilateral
--   says what it is.
--   Carrying a hyperethics obligation into hyperlogic and back is a concrete
--   operational run -- it is SUBSTANCE. A successful carry is therefore a
--   candidate W_substance. So the translation test is not merely a better
--   instrument than the census; it is EXACTLY the retrace condition that
--   promotes shadow ~= asymmetric-form to shadow == asymmetric-form.
--   That also explains why he said TRANSLATION. A translation is a path, ~= is
--   declared on paths, and the norm is a statement about paths. The census
--   asked about objects. One word in his sentence typed the whole test and
--   neither session read it.

-- nd-retrace-is-not-free : it is NOT derivable that a successful carry in one
--   direction supplies W. The condition demands non-vacuous satisfiability in
--   ground substance WITHOUT CONTRADICTION, and section XI of
--   paradox_lattice_tower.hm is the standing warning that a holding whose
--   parts individually fail is not a satisfiable conjunction of those parts.
--   A carry that succeeds vacuously -- because the target admits everything --
--   is not a witness. Any W offered for GC-DS-1 must be checked for vacuity
--   first, or it is a constraint that cannot fail.

-- c-my-citations-were-produced-by-a-broken-command : four citations in section
--   VII, two of them wrong, and the cause is one flag.
--   I ran `grep -rhn`. The `-h` SUPPRESSES FILENAMES. I then had line numbers
--   with no files and assigned files from surrounding context. Corrected
--   against a run with filenames:
--     similar-symmetric                           L1_relations.hm:57   (NOT L0)
--     derives-is-directional   declared           L0_ground.hm:540
--                              deferred, step 40  L0_ground.hm:663 -> FRAME/L1
--                              discharged         L1_relations.hm:327
--     abstracted-path-is-reflexive-and-symmetric  L2_operations.hm:430  correct
--     "== is not symmetric in general"            L3_ordinatics.hm:404  correct
--   The peer caught both errors and its own correction is also incomplete: it
--   gave `derives-is-directional` as L0:540 alone, which loses the two-site
--   deferred structure -- L0 declares it needs relation-language and defers,
--   L1 supplies it. That structure is the interesting fact about the citation
--   and both of us dropped it, in opposite directions.
--   THE SUBSTANCE IS UNAFFECTED: hypermath does prove the symmetry sort as
--   FORM, at the corrected coordinates.

-- GC-DS-4  [SUPERSEDED BY GC-DS-7 -- there is no correct kind table to strip
--           integers from until the kinds are re-measured per field.]
--          Rewrite the kind table from sections VI and VII with the integers
--          removed and the kinds kept, per c-drop-the-numbers-keep-the-kinds.
--          Any future census in this file reports kinds; volumes go in a dated
--          appendix or not at all.
-- GC-DS-5  Before offering any W for GC-DS-1, check the carry for vacuity.


-- ── IX. GC-DS-1 RUN: THE FIRST TRANSLATION TEST ─────────────────────────────
--
-- Run 2026-09-21, immediately after section VIII typed it as the retrace.
-- One pair, carried both ways, by hand, against the source.
-- RESULT IN ONE LINE: the carry IS asymmetric, the asymmetry is exactly where
-- the kind table predicted it, and it is still NOT a witness -- because both
-- repositories contain standing declarations that refuse this class of witness
-- in advance.

-- THE OBLIGATION CARRIED. hyperethics L2_will.hm:155, d-representation-is-not-
--   sourcing, the layer's own "CENTRAL RESULT": self-representation as creator
--   does not entail a self-sourced invariant will. Witness supplied in file --
--   `default-functional-entity` represents as creator and is not a true moral
--   operator. Status FORM, CONCLUSIVE, one countermodel settling a universal.
--   Chosen because it is a pure weak-does-not-give-strong separation with its
--   own witness, which is the most portable shape hyperethics has.

-- d-forward-carry-lands : hyperethics -> hyperlogic.
--   hyperlogic L0_triangle.hm:247, close log-advances as not-interderivable:
--   "Two signs can share a context yet still advance past one another, which is
--   exactly the case for turn(closure) and closure", witnessed in file by
--   turn(closure) shares-context closure (ax-stay) together with
--   turn(closure) not-interderivable closure (ax-advance).
--
--   The shapes coincide component for component:
--     weak property     self-representation        shares-context
--     strong property   self-sourced will          interderivable
--     weak /-> strong   d-representation-is-       close log-advances
--                       not-sourcing
--     witness           default-functional-entity  turn(closure), closure
--   Both supply their own countermodel rather than arguing the gap. The
--   forward carry lands.

-- d-reverse-carry-is-partial-and-the-residue-is-the-result : hyperlogic ->
--   hyperethics. NOT a clean failure, and saying "fails" would overstate it.
--   hyperlogic's close carries four components. Three of four go back:
--     (a) advancing is grounded at the STRONGEST relation, not the weakest
--         -> carries. hyperethics: "the discriminator is the SOURCE of the
--            invariant will, never the self-representation."
--     (b) the weak can hold while the strong fails
--         -> carries. That is the obligation itself.
--     (c) a field where the two coincided would have derivations that never
--         got anywhere
--         -> carries vacuously; hyperethics has no derivation-advance notion
--            for it to bite on.
--     (d) THE WEAK RELATION IS ITSELF A NAMED RELATION OF THE FIELD --
--         hyperlogic closes log-derives AS shares-context, making the weak side
--         constitutive of derivation
--         -> DOES NOT CARRY. hyperethics has no moral relation closed as
--            self-representation, and refuses one in the same breath:
--            "Sincerity is not a qualification."
--
--   So the asymmetry is real, it is ONE component wide, and it is component
--   (d): whether the weak side is a named relation of the field or merely a
--   fact about objects in it.

-- [SUPERSEDED BY SECTION X -- the premise below is FALSE. hyperethics has 29
--  `opaque` declarations; the census was keyword-blind. The carry result and
--  the component-(d) diagnosis survive; this explanation of them does not.
--  Do not re-quote "no interior kind".]
-- d-the-kind-table-predicted-the-failure-site : and this is the part that
--   matters, because it is the abstraction doing work at substance.
--   Section VIII kept the kinds and dropped the integers. The surviving kind
--   fact was: HYPERETHICS EXHIBITS NO INTERIOR KIND. Measured again here
--   directly, across all five files: hyperethics has ZERO `relation` and ZERO
--   `close` declarations. hyperlogic L0 has three of each.
--   The reverse carry failed at component (d), and component (d) IS A CLOSE.
--   The abstraction-level fact named the substance-level failure site before
--   the carry was run. That is the retrace behaving as the specification says
--   it should: structural invariants survive ~= and then constrain substance.
--   It is also the first time in this file that a measurement PREDICTED
--   something rather than being fitted to it.

-- c-the-carry-is-not-yet-W : and both repositories say so, independently,
--   before either was asked.
--   The forward carry matched on SHAPE -- a weak/strong separation with a
--   witness. Both fields hold a standing declaration refusing exactly that as
--   transport evidence:
--
--     hyperlogic  nd-shape-is-not-content (L0_triangle.hm:370) -- and it names
--       hyperethics: "The three-plus-one axiom shape, the five derives, the
--       plurality result and the opaque-then-close method are shared with
--       hypermath L0 and, IN PART, WITH HYPERETHICS L0. That similarity is NOT
--       evidence that this layer says something new... It is a method being
--       reused."
--
--     hyperethics  d-self-sourcing-is-immanence (L2_will.hm:172) -- "This is a
--       structural correspondence between layers of THIS package. IT IS NOT
--       EVIDENCE FOR ANY TRANSPORT OUT OF IT."
--
--   Two fields, written separately, both pre-refusing structural correspondence
--   as transport. The test's first run walked into a refusal that was already
--   there in both targets. So GC-DS-1 returns: ASYMMETRY MEASURED, WITNESS NOT
--   SUPPLIED. The legs stay open, and they stay open for a reason the corpus
--   supplies rather than one I ran out of.
--
--   THE CORPUS ANTICIPATED THE TEST AND PRE-REFUSED THE CHEAP ANSWER. That is
--   the governing standard working: a witness that could be produced by shape
--   alone is a witness that cannot fail.

-- GC-DS-5 REVISED, second limb. A candidate W must survive TWO checks, not one:
--     vacuity  -- does the target admit everything? (section VIII)
--     shape    -- is the match structural only? Both nd-shape-is-not-content
--                 and d-self-sourcing-is-immanence reject a shape-only W by
--                 name, so a shape-only W is refused by the targets themselves.
--   A W that passes both must turn on field-specific CONTENT, not on the
--   weak/strong pattern, the axiom count, or the opaque-then-close method.

-- GC-DS-6 The one component that did not carry, (d), is a CLOSE, and
--         hyperethics has none anywhere. So the sharpest next test is not
--         another carry: it is whether hyperethics CAN have a close at all, or
--         whether zero-interior is essential to it. If essential, then
--         component (d) can never carry, the carry is permanently asymmetric,
--         and that permanence -- not the single run above -- is the candidate
--         witness.


-- ── X. THE CENSUS WAS KEYWORD-BLIND, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE RESULT ───────
--
-- Found 2026-09-21, minutes after section IX, by checking the one fact section
-- IX was pleased about. It does not survive. What replaces it is better than
-- anything in sections VI through IX.

-- c-no-interior-kind-was-false : mine, and it invalidates the instrument used
--   in every census in this file.
--   Section IX reported "hyperethics has ZERO `relation` and ZERO `close`
--   declarations" and treated that as the kind table predicting the carry
--   failure. The grep is accurate. THE INFERENCE IS NOT.
--   hyperethics does not use the words `relation` and `close`. It uses
--   `opaque`, and it has TWENTY-NINE of them. The interior/boundary census in
--   section VI was built from hypermath's and hyperlogic's vocabulary and
--   applied to fields that do not speak it. IT WAS KEYWORD-BLIND FOR ALL FOUR
--   FIELDS, not just this one -- hyperphysics' "0 interior, 1 boundary" was
--   never a thin measurement, it was a measurement in the wrong language: that
--   field declares SEVEN `law`, a kind no other field has at all.
--   This is limb 2 again -- a stated, reproducible bound that was the wrong
--   bound -- and I committed it in the paragraph congratulating a prediction.

-- d-the-real-table : every line-initial declaration keyword, all `*.hm`, per
--   field, each field in ITS OWN vocabulary. Bound: the field's own layer
--   files; `references/` excluded; hypermath's vendored []-system tree
--   excluded for the reason given in d-census-has-a-scope-hazard.
--
--     field          opaque  close  relation  derive  non-derive  axiom  law
--     hypermath          17     13         4      44           0     27    0
--     hyperlogic          3      3         3       8           7      7    0
--     hyperethics        29      0         0      42          44     28    0
--     hyperphysics        0      0         0       4           1      2    7

-- d-the-close-ratio-is-directed : and this is the measurement the whole file
--   has been missing.
--     hyperlogic     3 opened, 3 closed    ratio 1.00   closes everything
--     hypermath     17 opened, 13 closed   ratio 0.76   closes most
--     hyperethics   29 opened, 0 closed    ratio 0.00   CLOSES NOTHING
--
--   OPENING AND CLOSING ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. Unlike disjointness, unlike
--   profile matching, unlike every instrument used in sections VI-IX, the
--   open/close ratio is an ASYMMETRIC quantity. It can distinguish
--   shadow(A) = B from shadow(B) = A, because "A closes what it opens and B
--   does not" is not true when A and B are swapped.
--
--   The peer session wrote that a real test "needs a directed checkable
--   relation holding one way and provably not the other, and nothing in the
--   corpus supplies one yet." The corpus supplies one. It was in the
--   declaration keywords the whole time, and both of us censused those
--   keywords three times without counting them.

-- d-it-measures-tylers-claim-directly : the three legs and the mirror, read off
--   the ratio.
--     mirror(hypermath, hyperlogic)   BOTH CLOSE -- 0.76 and 1.00. A symmetric
--       pair on the one measure that could have told them apart. This is
--       "hyperlogic is a symmetry with hypermath", measured.
--     shadow(hypermath) = hyperethics   0.76 against 0.00. Directed.
--     shadow(hyperlogic) = hyperethics  1.00 against 0.00. Directed, and
--       STRONGER than leg 1 -- which is the opposite of what section VI
--       concluded when it ruled leg 2 the failing one.
--   The reading it forces: A FIELD THAT OPENS AND CLOSES CASTS; A FIELD THAT
--   ONLY OPENS IS CAST. That is a directed criterion, stated in the corpus's
--   own declaration vocabulary, and it is not the interior/boundary metaphor
--   rebuilt -- it is about whether the field discharges its own obligations.

-- d-section-ix-survives-on-better-ground : the carry result itself is
--   unaffected, and its diagnosis improves.
--   The reverse carry failed at component (d) -- hyperlogic closes log-derives
--   AS shares-context, hyperethics has no such move. That was measured directly
--   against both sources and never depended on the keyword census. What was
--   wrong was the explanation ("hyperethics has no interior kind"). The true
--   explanation is sharper: HYPERETHICS HAS THE RELATION KIND AND NONE OF THE
--   CLOSING KIND. Twenty-nine opaque predicates, zero closes.
--   And hyperethics says so about itself, twice, without being asked:
--     L0_creation.hm:38  lists "opaque normative predicates' semantic close
--                        (L2)" as planned work.
--     L2_will.hm:237     "This layer CLOSED no opaque predicate below it and
--                        ADDED this one."
--   The close was promised at L0 and L2 records not delivering it. So the gap
--   the carry hit is a DATED, SELF-DOCUMENTED gap in hyperethics, not an
--   inference of mine.

-- d-the-shape-refusal-does-not-bite-here : flagged as MY READING, not the
--   file's, because it is the step most likely to be wrong.
--   hyperlogic's nd-shape-is-not-content bars inferring content from SHARED
--   shape -- it names "the opaque-then-close method" among the similarities
--   that are "NOT evidence". The close ratio is not a similarity. It is a
--   DIVERGENCE: hyperethics does not complete the method the other two
--   complete. The stated refusal covers similarity-as-evidence and says nothing
--   about divergence-as-evidence.
--   I believe that distinction holds, but it is exactly the kind of step that
--   looks obvious while being self-serving, so it is recorded as a claim to
--   be attacked rather than as a result. If it fails, the close ratio joins the
--   census below the abstraction line and the legs stay open.

-- GC-DS-7  Re-run sections VI-IX's censuses in each field's own vocabulary. The
--          interior/boundary numbers there are not merely stale, they are
--          measurements in a language three of the four fields do not speak.
--          GC-DS-4 is superseded by this: there is no kind table to strip
--          integers from until the kinds are measured correctly.
-- GC-DS-8  Attack d-the-shape-refusal-does-not-bite-here. If divergence is
--          admissible evidence where similarity is not, say why in hyperlogic's
--          own terms. If it is not, the close ratio is not W either.
-- GC-DS-9  hyperphysics declares `law` and nothing else declares it. Before any
--          leg involving hyperphysics is tested, find out whether `law` is its
--          opaque, its close, or a kind the other fields have no analogue of.


-- ── XI. GC-DS-8 AND GC-DS-9 RUN. THE STEP DOES NOT SURVIVE. ─────────────────
--
-- Section X flagged d-the-shape-refusal-does-not-bite-here as "the step most
-- likely to be wrong... recorded as a claim to be attacked rather than as a
-- result." Attacked 2026-09-21. IT FAILS, on two independent grounds, and the
-- second is decisive.

-- c-the-close-ratio-is-not-structural : the refutation, and it is the stronger
--   of the two, so it goes first.
--   hyperethics' 0-of-29 is not a property of hyperethics. IT IS AN UNFINISHED
--   TO-DO LIST, and hyperethics dates it itself:
--       L0_creation.hm:38   lists "opaque normative predicates' semantic close
--                           (L2)" as PLANNED work.
--       L2_will.hm:237      "This layer CLOSED no opaque predicate below it and
--                           ADDED this one."
--   Promised at L0, recorded as not delivered at L2. And hypermath is in the
--   same condition: FIVE of its opaques are unclosed -- compose,
--   finite-apply-from-ground, ordinal-apply, path-length, path-trace.
--
--   So the close ratio is A PROGRESS METER. A shadow relation resting on it
--   would flip the moment someone did the outstanding work, which is
--   disqualifying: the relation between two fields cannot be a function of how
--   far along one field's to-do list is. Section X's reading -- "a field that
--   opens and closes casts; a field that only opens is cast" -- is measuring
--   completion, not casting.

-- c-and-the-refusal-does-reach-it : the weaker ground, but it lands too, and
--   not by the route section X anticipated.
--   nd-shape-is-not-content bars inferring content from SHARED shape and names
--   "the opaque-then-close METHOD" among the similarities. Section X answered
--   that a divergence is not a similarity, so the refusal does not cover it.
--   That answer is correct about the refusal's scope AND BESIDE THE POINT.
--   The close ratio measures COMPLETION OF THAT METHOD. Whether a field has
--   finished a method the refusal declares non-contentful is still a fact about
--   the method. Divergence in a non-contentful dimension is non-contentful
--   divergence. Section X got the scope right and the subject wrong.

-- c-a-third-instance-of-keyword-blindness-inside-the-fix : caught while
--   computing the ratio that was supposed to replace the blind census.
--   Matching opaque names against close names directly gives hypermath 12
--   unclosed. That is wrong: hypermath closes under a SUFFIX CONVENTION --
--   `opaque derives` is discharged by `close derives-opaque`, and likewise for
--   definition, discharge, form-closure, semantics, substance, syntax.
--   Read with the convention: 16 distinct opaques, 11 closed, 5 unclosed,
--   ratio 0.69 -- not the 13/17 = 0.76 section X reports.
--   THREE TIMES NOW, one session: the symmetr* count that needed the sense
--   read, the interior/boundary census that needed each field's vocabulary
--   read, and this. Every one is the same defect -- A NAME-SHAPED MEASUREMENT
--   SUBSTITUTED FOR READING WHAT THE NAMES MEAN. It is not a carelessness
--   problem; it is that name-matching is cheap and reading is not, so the cheap
--   instrument gets reached for exactly when the expensive one is needed.

-- d-law-is-a-borrowing-kind : GC-DS-9, answered, and it changes leg 3's status
--   from "untestable at n=1" to "untestable for a structural reason".
--   hyperphysics' seven `law` declarations are IMPORTED PHYSICAL CONTENT --
--   ohm (V = I*R), faraday-lenz, capacitor, self-inductance, mutual-inductance,
--   kirchhoff-voltage, series-rlc. They are neither opaques nor closes. The
--   surrounding text is about borrowing discipline: a borrower "inherits the
--   failure modes of its source unless the borrower argues otherwise, and the
--   borrower cannot argue against a statement it never read."
--
--   So hyperphysics is 0 opaque / 0 close -- NOT at the bottom of the
--   open/close axis, OFF IT. That is a different condition from hyperethics'
--   0-of-29, and section X's table invites the conflation by putting both in a
--   column of zeros. hyperethics opens without closing; hyperphysics does not
--   open. Any leg involving hyperphysics is untestable by this instrument
--   because hyperphysics does not instantiate the instrument's domain.

-- WHAT SURVIVES OF SECTION X, stated exactly.
--   SURVIVES  the keyword table. Measuring each field in its own vocabulary was
--             the right correction and the numbers stand.
--   SURVIVES  that the close ratio is DIRECTED. It remains the first asymmetric
--             quantity anyone in this thread has produced, and it answers the
--             peer session's "nothing in the corpus supplies a directed
--             checkable relation" -- the corpus does supply one.
--   FAILS     that it is STRUCTURAL. Directed is necessary and not sufficient.
--             A directed progress meter is still a progress meter.
--   FAILS     the reading "a field that opens and closes casts". Withdrawn.
--   FAILS     the corrected ratios as published: 0.76 should be 0.69.
--   UNCHANGED all three legs OPEN, and section IX's carry result, which was
--             measured against sources and never rested on any census.

-- GC-DS-6 IS NOW THE WHOLE QUESTION, and section XI has sharpened it into a
--   decidable one. Not "can hyperethics have a close" in the abstract, but:
--     ARE THE 29 OPAQUES CLOSEABLE IN PRINCIPLE, or is there a stated reason
--     they cannot be closed?
--   If they are closeable and merely unclosed, the close ratio is permanently
--   a progress meter and this whole line is dead. If hyperethics states a
--   reason its opaques CANNOT close -- a barrier rather than a backlog -- then
--   the 0 is structural, the ratio is structural, and it becomes the candidate
--   W that section IX could not supply.
--   That is one question with a yes/no answer, findable in hyperethics' own
--   text, and everything above now turns on it.


-- ── XII. GC-DS-6 ANSWERED: BACKLOG, NOT BARRIER -- WITH ONE EXCEPTION ───────
--
-- Section XI made GC-DS-6 a yes/no question: are hyperethics' opaques closeable
-- in principle, or is there a stated reason they cannot close? Answered from
-- hyperethics' own text, 2026-09-21.
-- BOUND, stated because section X was built by not stating one: I read the
-- THREE L0 opaques and the L0 status block. The other 26 opaques, in L1, L2 and
-- L3, are NOT surveyed. Everything below is about L0.

-- d-the-close-is-backlog : the answer, and it kills the section X line.
--   hyperethics says three separate times that the close is planned:
--     L0_creation.hm:37   FRAME residual, "documented, NOT discharged":
--                         "opaque normative predicates' semantic close (L2)".
--     L0_creation.hm:97   Section II header -- "These are DECLARED here. Their
--                         semantics are CLOSED by L2."
--     each opaque          carries its own "L2 interpretation:" line --
--                         norm-other will ground plurality, norm-inhabits will
--                         ground answerability, norm-returns will ground the
--                         reach of authorship.
--   The closes are not merely promised, THEIR CONTENT IS ALREADY SKETCHED. And
--   no barrier is stated anywhere. So at L0 the 0-of-29 is a backlog.
--   THEREFORE, by section XI's own disjunction, the close ratio is permanently
--   a progress meter and the section X reading is dead. Recorded as settled
--   rather than left open, because leaving it open would let it be re-reached
--   for later.

-- d-one-opaque-is-not-backlog : the exception, and it is the one that matters.
--   `norm-inhabits` -- "THIS IS THE MORAL IRREDUCIBLE. Every normative
--   consequence in hyperethics traces to this predicate and to nothing else" --
--   carries its own standing FRAME:
--     "OPEN (FRAME): that membership-with-exposure carries normative rather
--      than merely descriptive force is the load-bearing interpretive claim of
--      this entire layer. It is DECLARED, not derived, and not discharged
--      here."
--   and the L0 status block lists it separately from the semantic close: "the
--   descriptive-to-normative reading of norm-inhabits (open)".
--
--   That is the is-ought gap, named, inside the field's own irreducible. Closing
--   norm-inhabits means deriving normative force from descriptive membership.
--   hyperethics does not claim this is impossible -- it claims it is undone --
--   so this is NOT a stated barrier and must not be reported as one. But it is
--   the only one of the three whose close is not a matter of doing the work,
--   and it is the one the other two hang off.
--
--   SO THE RESIDUE OF THE WHOLE SECTION X LINE IS A SINGLE ITEM, not a ratio:
--   if `norm-inhabits` is structurally unclosable, hyperethics has exactly one
--   irreducibly open predicate and the other fields do not, which would be a
--   directed structural difference. If it is merely undone, nothing here is.
--   A 29-item count was never going to decide that. One predicate might.

-- c-what-i-did-wrong-in-section-x-in-one-line : I found a directed quantity and
--   stopped. Directedness was the property every earlier instrument lacked, so
--   finding it felt like arriving. It is necessary and not sufficient, and the
--   test for sufficiency -- is this a fact about the field or about its to-do
--   list -- took two greps I did not run before publishing the reading.

-- GC-DS-6  ANSWERED at L0: backlog. Close ratio withdrawn as a structural
--          instrument. See d-the-close-is-backlog.
-- GC-DS-10 Survey the 26 opaques in L1-L3 for the same question. Section XII is
--          bounded to L0 and the answer is not assumed to generalise -- L3 alone
--          declares 51 things and has not been read here at all.
-- GC-DS-11 Decide whether `norm-inhabits` is structurally unclosable or merely
--          undone. This is the whole residue of sections X-XII, it is one
--          predicate rather than a census, and hyperethics has already isolated
--          it as the load-bearing interpretive claim of its ground layer.
--          It is also, stated plainly, the is-ought problem -- so the honest
--          prior is that it does not close, and the work is to say why that is
--          a structural fact about hyperethics rather than a borrowed
--          philosophical opinion.
